// REJECTED — a type name written in an arrow annotation is NOT a fresh type
// parameter (roadmap item 75(a) rule G, guarding the item-75(c) hygiene hole).
//
// An arrow is an expression, not a signature, so it never quantifies. A name in
// its parameter or return annotation resolves to a type parameter of the
// ENCLOSING `fn`, else to a declared type, else to an ordinary opaque nominal
// type that unifies with nothing. Never to a new type parameter — if `Q` here
// were quantified, this arrow would wildcard at every call site, a one-letter
// typo would silently type-check again, and unlike a `fn` there is no `[T]`
// list anywhere for an author to turn the heuristic off with
// (docs/generics.md, "An explicit list turns the implicit heuristic OFF").
//
// So `Q` is opaque: `f` is a `(Q) -> Q`, and `Int` does not flow into `Q`.
// rust reports E0308 and javac "incompatible types".
//
// Legal spellings:
//   * mean a concrete type:  `let f = (x: Int): Int => x`
//   * mean the enclosing fn's type parameter:
//       `fn id[T](x: T) -> T { let f = (v: T): T => v  return f(x) }`
//
// expected error:
//   argument 1 of `f` expects `Q`, got `Int`

fn takes_str(s: Str) -> Str {
  return s
}

fn demo() -> Str {
  let f = (x: Q): Q => x
  return takes_str(f(1))
}
